                                                                international conference on Finclusion 2019 at G L Bajaj
                                                                Institute of

                                                                Management and Research.

                                                            </li>

                                                        </ul>

                                                    </div>

                                                    <div id="Button4" class="tabcontent links">

                                                        <p class="facpara brfprofile">
                                                            Marketing, at its core, is not a subject to be memorized —
                                                            it is a discipline to be experienced. In a world where
                                                            consumer behavior shifts with a swipe and brand narratives
                                                            are built in real time, the classroom must cease to be a
                                                            passive theatre of instruction. It must become a living
                                                            laboratory where students think, argue, experiment, fail,
                                                            and ultimately discover. This conviction shapes every
                                                            pedagogical choice I make.
                                                        </p>

                                                        <p class="factitle">Case-based pedagogy</p>

                                                        <p class="facpara">
                                                            Case-based pedagogy forms the bedrock of my teaching
                                                            practice. However, I deploy cases not merely as storytelling
                                                            tools but as structured cognitive challenges. Students are
                                                            deliberately placed in conditions of ambiguity — incomplete
                                                            data, competing stakeholder interests, ethical grey zones —
                                                            mirroring the messiness of real marketing decisions. Whether
                                                            it is a fast-moving consumer goods brand losing shelf
                                                            relevance or a digital-native startup confronting its first
                                                            brand crisis, the case room becomes a space for disciplined
                                                            reasoning under pressure. The goal is not the "right" answer
                                                            but the ability to construct a defensible, data-informed
                                                            argument — a skill that distinguishes an analyst from a
                                                            manager.
                                                        </p>

                                                        <p class="factitle">Role plays</p>

                                                        <p class="facpara">
                                                            Knowing about a negotiation and being in one are entirely
                                                            different cognitive experiences. I use structured role plays
                                                            to close this gap. Students assume the identities of brand
                                                            managers, media planners, agency creatives, and even
                                                            dissatisfied consumers. This embodied learning cultivates
                                                            empathy — an undervalued competency in marketing — and
                                                            compels students to pressure-test their assumptions from
                                                            within unfamiliar perspectives. A student who has once
                                                            "played" a skeptical retail buyer develops a permanently
                                                            different instinct for trade marketing strategy.
                                                        </p>

                                                        <p class="factitle">Asynchronous discussion forums</p>

                                                        <p class="facpara">
                                                            Asynchronous discussion forums serve as intellectual commons
                                                            outside the classroom. I design prompts that are
                                                            deliberately provocative — a controversial campaign, an
                                                            emerging market entry dilemma, a brand authenticity debate —
                                                            to ensure that quieter voices, who often carry the most
                                                            considered perspectives, contribute alongside the vocal few.
                                                            This practice cultivates the habit of reflective commentary:
                                                            students learn that opinion, unanchored by reasoning, is
                                                            noise. Structured argument, by contrast, is currency.
                                                        </p>

                                                        <p class="factitle">Field projects</p>

                                                        <p class="facpara">
                                                            Perhaps the most transformative element of my pedagogy is
                                                            the field project. Students venture beyond the campus —
                                                            visiting retail outlets, interviewing consumers, mapping
                                                            competitive landscapes, and observing point-of-sale behavior
                                                            firsthand. There is a particular intellectual awakening that
                                                            occurs when a student's carefully constructed theory meets
                                                            the obstinate reality of a consumer who "doesn't behave
                                                            rationally." Field projects teach epistemic humility
                                                            alongside market intelligence, producing graduates who lead
                                                            with curiosity rather than assumption.
                                                        </p>

                                                        <p class="factitle">Entrepreneurial marketing modules</p>

                                                        <p class="facpara">
                                                            In my entrepreneurial marketing modules, student teams
                                                            conceive, validate, and prototype new products or services —
                                                            from ideation through concept testing to a simulated market
                                                            launch. This end-to-end NPD experience forces integrative
                                                            thinking: Who is the target segment? What is the value
                                                            proposition? How does the product communicate its promise at
                                                            the shelf or on the screen? Presenting a physical or digital
                                                            prototype to a panel of industry practitioners — rather than
                                                            just a professor — raises the stakes and sharpens thinking
                                                            in ways that no examination can replicate.
                                                        </p>

                                                        <p class="factitle">Flipped classroom model</p>

                                                        <p class="facpara">
                                                            Lecture-as-default is a pedagogical inheritance I have
                                                            consciously rejected. In my flipped classroom model,
                                                            conceptual content is delivered through curated pre-reads,
                                                            short video modules, and guided frameworks before the
                                                            session. Class time is then reserved for what it does best:
                                                            debate, synthesis, application, and peer learning. Students
                                                            arrive not as blank slates but as prepared participants. The
                                                            classroom conversation deepens accordingly, and I find that
                                                            students develop greater ownership over their learning when
                                                            they are architects of in-class discourse rather than its
                                                            passive recipients.
                                                        </p>

                                                        <p class="factitle">Teaching philosophy</p>

                                                        <p class="facpara">
                                                            Across all these methods runs a single thread: the belief
                                                            that marketing education must produce not merely skilled
                                                            technicians but reflective practitioners — professionals who
                                                            combine analytical rigor with human empathy, and commercial
                                                            ambition with ethical awareness. In an era of algorithmic
                                                            precision and attention fragmentation, the students who will
                                                            lead are those who can still ask the harder question: What
                                                            does this brand owe its customer, and does it deliver?
                                                        </p>

                                                        <p class="facpara">
                                                            That question, I hope, stays with them long after the last
                                                            class.
                                                        </p>

                                                        <p class="facpara">
                                                            The most gratifying moment in teaching is not when a student
                                                            answers correctly — it is when a student questions
                                                            confidently. When a young manager walks into their first
                                                            brand review and instinctively asks not just what the
                                                            numbers say but why the consumer behaved that way, the
                                                            classroom has done its work. My teaching innovations are not
                                                            techniques adopted for novelty; they are deliberate
                                                            architectures of experience, designed to ensure that
                                                            learning outlasts the semester. Marketing will keep changing
                                                            — its channels, its metrics, its vocabulary. What must not
                                                            change is the graduate's capacity to think originally, act
                                                            ethically, and lead with both conviction and humility. That,
                                                            ultimately, is the standard I hold myself to — not just as
                                                            an educator, but as a practitioner who believes that the
                                                            best marketing begins with the deepest understanding of
                                                            people.
                                                        </p>

